WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.853 --> 00:00:08.537
At any other time and place, an injured knee would be no big deal.

2
00:00:09.158 --> 00:00:14.181
But weather expert Dar Gibson was working at the South Pole, in the middle of winter, when he

3
00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:17.003
fell and badly damaged a tendon in his left knee.

4
00:00:17.723 --> 00:00:24.648
None of Gibson's 51 coworkers at the National Science Foundation research station were qualified to carry out the

5
00:00:24.748 --> 00:00:30.012
operation needed to repair the tendon, and the station's clinic was not equipped for surgery.

6
00:00:30.804 --> 00:00:37.169
The extreme cold and total darkness of Antarctica in July meant that flying him out─or flying a surgeon

7
00:00:37.249 --> 00:00:38.950
in─was out of the question.

8
00:00:40.131 --> 00:00:46.316
The station doctor told Gibson the bad news: without surgery, he would never walk properly again.

9
00:00:46.336 --> 00:00:52.700
A solution to Gibson's dilemma arrived, however, in the form of the latest medical technology.

10
00:00:53.421 --> 00:01:00.746
A combination of computers and an advanced communication network allowed two physicians to help repair Gibson's knee from

11
00:01:00.786 --> 00:01:05.349
their office in Boston, Massachusetts, many thousands of kilometers away.

12
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.552
The physician at the South Pole research station, Dr.

13
00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:12.574
Timothy Pollard, prepared Gibson for the procedure.

14
00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:19.899
Then he and a computer network specialist slipped on operating smocks and masks and turned to face the

15
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:26.583
cameras that were beaming the scene to an office in Boston, where a surgeon and an anesthesiologist watched

16
00:01:26.663 --> 00:01:33.642
closely. An anesthesiologist is a doctor who is trained to administer the drugs that put a patient to

17
00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:37.643
sleep and who carefully monitors the patient during surgery.

18
00:01:39.003 --> 00:01:44.505
Using the high-speed connection, the Boston doctors examined x-rays of Gibson's knee.

19
00:01:45.645 --> 00:01:47.546
Under their expert guidance, Dr.

20
00:01:47.586 --> 00:01:52.607
Pollard cut into Gibson's knee, sutured the damaged tendon, and closed the incision.

21
00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:59.081
The two-hour operation was a success, and it is an example of a new trend in medical care

22
00:01:59.141 --> 00:02:00.662
known as telemedicine.

23
00:02:01.642 --> 00:02:06.064
Telemedicine is, however, more than just talking someone through an operation.

24
00:02:06.804 --> 00:02:12.647
In 2001, a pair of doctors operated on a woman in France─while they were in New York City!

25
00:02:13.447 --> 00:02:18.969
The pair, one Canadian and one of French origin, didn't just tell another doctor what to do.

26
00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.952
They actually performed the operation, removing the patient's gallbladder by remote control.

27
00:02:25.864 --> 00:02:33.067
They used a computerized control panel and a giant television screen, as well as robotic arms located in

28
00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:35.508
the operating room in Strasbourg, France.

29
00:02:36.549 --> 00:02:43.412
The communications link was a private, high-speed line, which is far more reliable than an ordinary Internet connection.

30
00:02:44.572 --> 00:02:52.876
The transatlantic operation, which was called "Operation Lindberg" after Charles Lindberg, the pilot who flew the first transatlantic

31
00:02:52.936 --> 00:02:56.198
flight, still needed the assistance of a doctor in France.

32
00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:03.363
He made four razor-thin cuts in the patient's stomach and inflated it with carbon dioxide to give the

33
00:03:03.403 --> 00:03:05.304
robotic hands room to operate.

34
00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:10.268
The surgery itself, however, was performed by the doctors in New York.

35
00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:16.512
Each time they moved one of the instruments in their hands, the robot in France made an identical

36
00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:18.734
move about a fifth of a second later.

37
00:03:19.555 --> 00:03:21.136
Within an hour, they were done.

38
00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:28.402
Operation Lindberg was an experiment, and the procedure cost thousands of dollars more than a normal operation.

39
00:03:29.262 --> 00:03:33.763
But many doctors expect that one day such procedures will become routine.

40
00:03:34.844 --> 00:03:36.144
Already, the U.S.

41
00:03:36.184 --> 00:03:42.326
government has approved a computerized, robotic surgical system, called da Vinci, for use in hospitals.

42
00:03:43.226 --> 00:03:49.528
The da Vinci system allows a doctor to operate on a patient's internal organs without opening up the

43
00:03:49.588 --> 00:03:56.591
entire chest─the cuts have to be large enough only for a minuscule camera and tools, which are much

44
00:03:56.651 --> 00:03:58.392
smaller than a pair of human hands.

45
00:03:59.592 --> 00:04:05.035
This reduces the stress on the patient, speeds healing, and reduces the chance of infection.

46
00:04:05.835 --> 00:04:12.719
The surgeon watches a screen that shows inside the patient on a computer and manipulates hand-sized controls that

47
00:04:12.759 --> 00:04:14.680
direct the microsurgery instruments.

48
00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:21.083
The da Vinci system was designed for operations in which the patient and medical team are in the

49
00:04:21.123 --> 00:04:21.723
same room.

50
00:04:22.444 --> 00:04:28.487
There is no reason, however, why the patient and doctor couldn't be separated by an entire continent or

51
00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:32.169
ocean. The patient could even be on a space station.

52
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:38.953
The most common use of telemedicine, and the reason it was developed, is for medical care in remote

53
00:04:38.993 --> 00:04:44.276
locations, like the Arctic tundra of Alaska or the Andes Mountains of Chile.

54
00:04:45.192 --> 00:04:52.254
Governments cannot always afford to give every community the latest medical equipment, but sometimes they can find the

55
00:04:52.314 --> 00:04:59.877
funds for communications technology, which can be used for long-distance education and official state business as well as

56
00:04:59.937 --> 00:05:08.899
telemedicine. Computers are much less expensive than airplane flights, and for patients suffering severe injuries, evacuation by airplane

57
00:05:08.959 --> 00:05:10.260
is not always a good idea.

58
00:05:11.164 --> 00:05:18.706
In countries like Canada and Australia, where great distances separate population centers and hospitals and where clinics in

59
00:05:18.746 --> 00:05:25.468
remote areas have difficulty attracting qualified medical specialists, telemedicine is now well established.

60
00:05:26.788 --> 00:05:33.170
In some cases, all that is needed for telemedicine is a good cell phone camera to transmit images

61
00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:39.592
of a patient's injuries to an office, clinic, or hospital, either nearby or across the country.

62
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.902
If the images are of high enough quality, a specialist may be able to prescribe a short-term treatment

63
00:05:47.402 --> 00:05:52.163
while the patient waits for the next chance to visit a doctor, or for the doctor to visit

64
00:05:52.203 --> 00:05:52.803
the patient.

65
00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:56.984
Furthermore, camera phones are getting better and better each year.

66
00:05:58.045 --> 00:06:03.126
Often, a trained nurse and someone who knows how to handle the communications gear are all that is

67
00:06:03.186 --> 00:06:06.327
required to bring faraway expertise to where it is needed.

68
00:06:07.247 --> 00:06:10.348
Telemedicine is not just changing the way patients are treated.

69
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:13.181
It is also changing the way doctors work.

70
00:06:13.942 --> 00:06:22.106
With a high-speed Internet connection and a state-of-the-art computer, it is now possible for radiologists─physicians who specialize in

71
00:06:22.166 --> 00:06:28.710
reading x-ray images, CAT scans, and MRI photographs─to work from home or even from a hotel room.

72
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:35.193
Other doctors, meanwhile, frequently use videoconferencing equipment to consult one another.

73
00:06:36.074 --> 00:06:42.557
School nurses, battlefield medics, and family doctors in country clinics can use satellite telephones to talk with their

74
00:06:42.577 --> 00:06:46.859
colleagues in metropolitan areas, all thanks to the latest technology.

75
00:06:47.799 --> 00:06:54.502
Not long ago, many people suffering from serious illnesses and injuries could only dream of receiving the highest

76
00:06:54.562 --> 00:06:59.524
quality medical care in their hometowns, or even in the comfort of their own homes.

77
00:07:00.344 --> 00:07:05.206
Yet that's what telemedicine is offering─the return of the old-fashioned house call.

78
00:07:06.046 --> 00:07:09.848
The only difference is, with telemedicine, the visit is virtual.
